{ Mar del Plata - the 9.Ne1 main line. ...f4 gains space and dislodges Be3. ...g5 supports the advanced pawn; ...Ng6 brings the e7 knight toward the king. These moves make sense because the center is locked. }
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5
Ne7 9. Ne1 Nd7 10. f3 f5 11. Be3 f4 12. Bf2 g5 13. Nd3 Ng6
{ Selected anchor ends here. Coordinate ...Nf6, ...h5 and queen or rook access before sacrificing. White will often play c5, so estimate whether you can tolerate a queenside file opening. White resource: Pawns alone do not mate. If the pieces cannot join, ...g4 may merely open lines against your own king. }
*

